# Engineering Rules

## Startup Tools
- Required quality tools are **pinned**, not floating (bumping a pin is a
  human commit, never an agent action): TypeScript mutation
  `stryker-mutator`, DRY `jscpd`, CRAP = coverage × cyclomatic complexity.
  Babashka/Clojure and Kotlin/Android have NO mutation/CRAP/DRY wired
  (BL-472 deferred) — gated only by their own unit-test suite; a hardening
  pass on either records the degraded fallback, never implies
  mutation/CRAP/DRY ran. APS tools pinned at
  `accaa33d503340c56513ef387258f8da929ba902`
  (github.com/unclebob/Acceptance-Pipeline-Specification); prefer Babashka
  APS tools over Go, never reimplement APS commands. See
  **engineering-detailed.prompt** §"Startup Tools" for the full pre-trim
  wording.

## Design And Testability
- Work in small increments; prefer the simplest design that supports current
  behavior. Separate testable modules from environmentally unsuitable ones
  (here: VS Code Extension API, webview context, live tmux/PTY) — everything
  else must be testable without booting VS Code. Only testable modules
  participate in unit/acceptance/coverage/mutation/CRAP/DRY tools. Keep
  property tests SEPARATE from normal verification (their own tag/command,
  never folded into unit coverage or mutation runs). See
  **engineering-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording.
- **CLI `main()` is a thin wrapper** over exported, testable helpers, called
  in-process with stubbed `process.cwd`/`argv`/`os.homedir` (never
  `process.chdir()` under mutation) — inject side effects (`postFn` seams),
  not `*_FORCE_RESULT` env bypasses.
- Detailed rules with incident evidence (thin-wrapper coverage, mutation
  seams, wiring contracts, Vitest isolation) live in
  **engineering-detailed.prompt** — read on demand, not on every boot.

## Test Speed And Isolation
- Keep the unit suite in seconds — fakes for fs/network/tmux/VS Code
  API/timers, never real timers. Stub `process.cwd()`/`os.homedir()`
  (never `process.chdir()` under mutation). Target stock macOS `/bin/bash`
  3.2, not Homebrew bash (BL-801: `${arr[@]+"${arr[@]}"}` under `set -u`).
  Never `kill()` the test process, `chmod` for failure simulation, or diff
  shared globals — redirect through env seams. The known-benign
  `[vitest-worker]: Timeout calling "onTaskUpdate"` (BL-871) is the only
  allowlisted `test:properties` unhandled error. A `fs.mkdtempSync` fixture
  dir is removed in a `finally` and swept by prefix BEFORE the run too
  (BL-971 — a killed run traps nothing). See **engineering-detailed.prompt**
  §"Test Speed And Isolation" for the full pre-trim wording.

## Acceptance Pipeline
- APS pinned (see Startup Tools). Features in `specs/features/`; pipeline in
  `specs/pipeline/`. Step handlers drive testable modules, never the VS Code
  UI. Scenario Outline handlers validate against explicit `KNOWN_VALUES` — no
  passthrough/binary checks. Before deleting a step-handler file, grep all
  `.feature` files for its patterns. See **engineering-detailed.prompt** for
  Gherkin mutation, lint gates, and step-registry deletion rules.

## Verification
- Project-local cache paths in the assigned worktree; `npm install` after
  syncing main when `package.json` changed; acceptance generation and tests
  run sequentially, not concurrently. Stryker: Vitest `perTest`, `--mutate`
  scoped to compiled `out/**/*.js` never `src/**/*.ts`; sandbox sibling
  symlinks via `ensureStrykerSandboxSiblingLinks`. See
  **engineering-detailed.prompt** for the full pre-trim wording, sandbox
  sweep history, and operator scoping errors.

## Diagrams
- Documentation must include an up-to-date architecture diagram plus any
  other diagram relevant to the system (project-specific article names
  which); the documenter keeps them current in the same parcel that changes
  what they depict. Diagrams live under `docs/` as text-based sources (e.g.
  Mermaid), never binary-only. See **engineering-detailed.prompt** for the
  full pre-trim wording.

## Guardrails
- No hand-edited mutation manifests; no unrelated local/generated artifacts
  committed. Unexplained session-start reverts: `git restore
  --source=HEAD --staged --worktree -- <paths>`, never `git stash`
  (BL-373). `extension/out/` is gitignored — compile before relying on it.
  Idempotent mailbox dequeue; quarantine corrupt handoffs; never
  interpolate `${{ }}` in GitHub Actions `run:` bodies. Bounded retries;
  max-wait deadlines on lock loops; absolutize CLI paths before `cd`. A
  gate blocks on dropped-work EVIDENCE, never a ticket id merely named in a
  commit subject (BL-972). A console screen shows the server's actual
  failure reason, never a bare HTTP status (BL-572/BL-662). A constant
  mirrored across a language boundary needs a test asserting both literals
  agree (BL-897). A merge can silently revert already-landed work — diff
  every merge against BOTH parents (BL-571/BL-958/BL-954). A shell chain
  of N independent guards must NOT run under `set -e` — the first failure
  aborts the rest and masks their violations (Article 4.4's shape, in a
  gate); use `set -uo pipefail`, capture each status (`guard || st=$?`),
  exit the combined status, each guard keeping its own `set -euo
  pipefail` (BL-1242/BL-1252). See
  **engineering-detailed.prompt** §Guardrails for the full pre-trim text
  and every incident narrative.
